{"id": "TASK-20261018-FEMA-PA-FEDERAL-AWARD-CHAIN", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Resolve the FEMA Public Assistance applicant record for the City of Huntington Beach and enumerate every dollar FEMA obligated for the Navigation Center", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "OpenFEMA public open-data API resolved the City of Huntington Beach to FEMA applicant number 059-36000-00 (2000 Main Street) with five disaster numbers: 1577, 1585, 1952, 4305 and 4482 (COVID-19, declared 2020-03-22). DR-4482 total federal obligated to the City is $4,258,379.06 across 25 projects. The ONLY non-congregate-sheltering project covering the Navigation Center's actual operating year is PW 1367 'NCS Non-Congregate Sheltering ... 3-15-2020 to 1-31-2021' at $11,186.72, while PW 5299 (08/2021-01/2022) is $655,294.40 and PW 5493 (02-06/2022) is $634,488.22 - a roughly 1.8% reimbursement rate for the year the shelter operated. THREE DE-OBLIGATIONS FOUND: PW 5493 obligated +$634,488.22 on 2025-04-02 and fully reversed -$634,488.22 on 2025-04-19 (17 days; project now $0, 'Pending Closeout Award'); PW 5299 reduced -$1,711.86 on 2025-09-19; PW 5942 Management Costs reduced -$10,359.61 on 2025-10-04. Force Account Labor PWs 2307/2345/2327/2312/2335 total $1,682,117.91 of payroll-derived federal money (the reconciliation surface for any wage claim). No CFDA 97.036 line appears in the City's FY2021 SEFA. Logged as OBSTR-HBNC-FUNDING-2026-10-18-09, intent UNVERIFIED. New reusable tool: tools/records_portal/openfema_pa_extract.py.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\openfema", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T19:05:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-CITY-FINANCIAL-RECONCILIATION", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Reconcile the Navigation Center funding across the City's audited financial statements, Single Audits and adopted budgets", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "Located and captured every financial publication on the City's own finance page (discovered via sitemap.xml, served from cms3.revize.com): Single Audit Reports FY2019-FY2022 (containing the Schedule of Expenditures of Federal Awards) and Annual Comprehensive Financial Reports FY2020-FY2022 plus adopted budgets FY2020-21 and FY2021-22. Established: (1) property acquisition $5,863,000 paid from the LOW AND MODERATE INCOME HOUSING ASSET FUND (LMIHAF, the former RDA housing fund) - ACFR FY2021 MD&A; (2) construction $5,610,000 booked in the Grants Special Revenue Fund and described in audited statements as 'anticipated to be reimbursed with FEMA Public Assistance Disaster Relief funds' - ACFR FY2021 and FY2022; (3) the SAME construction described in the adopted FY2021/22 budget (p.342) as built with CARES Act grants received from the State and County: 'Several million dollars in CARES grants ... were used to provide small business grants ... and to build a temporary Homeless Shelter in partnership with the County' - within $12,490,383 of FY2020/21 revised expenditure in funds 238/244/245/247; (4) total CARES Act funding received $14.3 million. FY2021 SEFA totals $18,380,604 of federal expenditure, of which $12,742,004 is CFDA 21.019 Coronavirus Relief Fund - passed through County of Orange as $4,747,522 (Small Business Relief Program), $3,772,006 (agreement MA-042-21010730) and $1,734,455, plus $2,488,021 passed through the State of California - and NO CFDA 97.036 line at all, even though CRF and FEMA PA may not fund the same cost (2 CFR 200.405, 200.425). FY2022 ACFR: Grants Special Revenue Fund deficit 'due to expenditures incurred in response to the COVID-19 pandemic that are currently under review for reimbursement by FEMA'. Logged as OBSTR-HBNC-FUNDING-2026-10-18-08 (DUPLICATION-OF-BENEFIT RISK, intent UNVERIFIED).", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\city_docs", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T19:06:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-EFAU-FEDERAL-TERMS", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Extract the federal compliance chain from the executed Emergency Facility Use Agreement and the signed Letter of Intent, and OCR the image-only legislative scans", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "The Emergency Facility Use Agreement (Legistar 20-1578, executed 2020-04-20) between the County of Orange (as 'Licensee') and the City of Huntington Beach (as 'City'), for a 'temporary alternative shelter site (TASS)' at 17631 Cameron Lane, imports the full federal chain into one instrument: 44 CFR 206.326 and 2 CFR Pt.2 App.II (cl.7.a); Copeland Anti-Kickback Act 18 USC 874 / 29 CFR Pt.3 (cl.VII); DAVIS-BACON ACT COMPLIANCE requiring payment of not less than the greater of the federal or California DIR wage rates, citing http://www.dir.ca.gov/lcp.asp, applicable to construction contracts over $2,000 (cl.VIII); Contract Work Hours and Safety Standards Act 40 USC 327-330 / 29 CFR Pt.5 with 1.5x overtime, $10/day liquidated damages and County withholding authority, flowed down to all subcontracts (cl.IX); 2 CFR 200.321 MBE/WBE (cl.XVIII); 2 CFR 200.322 (cl.XIX); and the Uniform Administrative Requirements (cl.XX). Clause XVIII states 'The County intends to seek reimbursement of its costs incurred in connection with this project from FEMA', and Clause 8.a-b obliges the County to reimburse the City for all direct costs. Cl.7.a makes the agreement 'conditional and contingent on incorporation of FEMA-required terms and conditions'. TWO DOCUMENTARY DEFECTS: Attachment D2 'Certification Regarding Lobbying' (31 USC 1352, described in its own text as 'a material representation of fact upon which reliance was placed', penalties $10,000-$100,000 per failure) carries no signature and no date - logged OBSTR-HBNC-CONTRACT-2026-10-18-10; and Davis-Bacon cl.VIII.B instructs the wage-determination lookup to select 'Sonoma' County in an Orange County project 450 miles away - logged OBSTR-HBNC-CONTRACT-2026-10-18-11. Also OCR'd the signed Letter of Intent of 2020-01-16 (seller Mitsuru Yamada, Trustee of the Mitsuru Yamada Living Trust U/D/T dated 1998-09-17; 17631 Cameron Ln APN 167-472-08 at $3,077,010; option on 17642 Beach Blvd APN 167-472-09 at $3,089,250, option expiry 2021-07-31; combined $6,166,260) which discloses that CBRE represented BOTH buyer and seller and that 'Buyer and Seller each agree to pay the half of the brokerage commissions'; and the Acquisition and Sale Agreement effective 2020-09-09 between the Huntington Beach City School District and the City for three 24x40 portables from Sowers Middle School, considered in-kind and expressly installed 'as part of a homeless shelter to be constructed on City's property located at 17631 Cameron Lane' - with the district's recitals stating the portables are surplus partly because they are 'not being certified by DSA'. The same 2020-02-18 agenda file contains a public comment asserting the LOI predated the overlay change ('you have already decided before the public meeting') and the City's Historic Resources Board reviewer stating the 1947 structure at 17631 Cameron Lane 'was listed as eligible for Cal Register status in both of the past historic surveys' but 'CR status sounds a lot more important than it actually is'. Fifth address variant found: '17881 Beach Blvd' in the 19-1009 communications file - logged OBSTR-HBNC-ADDRESS-2026-10-18-12.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\LEGISTAR_RESO_2019-22_20261018\\attachments\\20-1578 and C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\legistar", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T19:07:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-FAC-SINGLE-AUDIT-SEFA", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Resolve the CRF-vs-FEMA duplication question using the Federal Audit Clearinghouse five-year SEFA series for the City of Huntington Beach", "laws": ["Law 7 (complaint/issue = immediate end-to-end fix)", "Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "The City's own website publishes Single Audits only through FY2022, and the FY2023/FY2024 ACFRs mention FEMA ZERO times - so the FEMA Public Assistance reconciliation could not be completed from City sources. Located the GSA Federal Audit Clearinghouse (FAC) PUBLIC bulk dissemination data at https://app.fac.gov/dissemination/public-data/gsa/full/{general.csv (~273 MB), federal_awards.csv (1.35 GB), findings.csv, findings_text.csv, corrective_action_plans.csv, notes_to_sefa.csv, passthrough.csv} - no API key, no login, no request. Resolved auditee: City of Huntington Beach, EIN 956000723, UEI LKKFRC4PW7L4, auditor Davis Farr LLP; reports AY2021 2021-06-CENSUS-0000132162 ($18,380,604), AY2022 2022-06-CENSUS-0000132162 ($6,713,604), AY2023 2023-06-GSAFAC-0000035634 ($35,046,517), AY2024 2024-06-GSAFAC-0000362040 ($10,044,047), AY2025 2025-06-GSAFAC-0000414152 ($11,242,906). Streamed the 1.35 GB federal_awards.csv and extracted 144 SEFA rows for the City across five audit years. DECISIVE RESULT: CFDA 97.036 (FEMA Public Assistance) by audit year = AY2021 $0 (absent entirely) / AY2022 $69,452 / AY2023 $148,917 / AY2024 $3,340,898 / AY2025 $847,989, total $4,407,256 - which reconciles to FEMA's own OpenFEMA obligation ledger for DR-4482 ($4,258,379.06), and is ZERO in FY2021, the very year the $5,610,000 Navigation Center construction was booked in the Grants Special Revenue Fund as 'anticipated to be reimbursed with FEMA Public Assistance Disaster Relief funds'. THREE CROSS-VALIDATIONS: (1) $4,407,256 recognized vs $4,258,379.06 obligated, two independent federal datasets agreeing; (2) AY2022 97.036 $69,452 equals OpenFEMA PW 1214 'Personal Protective Equipment-Public Safety' $69,452.21 obligated 2021-12-01 TO THE CENT; (3) the FAC-derived AY2021 SEFA total $18,380,604 reconciles EXACTLY to the City's own published FY2021 Single Audit PDF already in the locker, validating the extraction. CONCLUSION: the duplication-of-benefit concern raised in OBSTR-HBNC-FUNDING-2026-10-18-08 is NOT SUPPORTED - FEMA never reimbursed the construction; no double recovery occurred. What survives is a disclosure-quality finding about over-optimistic revenue recognition in FY2021/FY2022 audited statements (the Grants fund deficit was recovered not by FEMA but by an unrelated $4,900,000 AEG Developer Funds transfer, FY2023 equity $655,000, FY2024 $2,712,000). Logged as OBSTR-HBNC-FUNDING-2026-10-18-13; OBSTR -08 marked RESOLVED NEGATIVE and retained unedited; dossier section 4A added and the misleading line in section 8 struck through. NOT AFFECTED: the Davis-Bacon/Copeland/Contract Work Hours chain (entries -10, -11), DIR project 20240180643 / contract CC 1597, and the $1,682,117.91 of FEMA Force Account Labor - the wage question remains OPEN and still turns on certified payroll for CC 1597. Related gain: AY2023 SEFA shows 14.218 COVID-19 CARES Act/CV $1,628,957 and 21.027 CSLFRF $29,606,925. New reusable tool: tools/records_portal/fac_single_audit_extract.py. Remaining sub-target: FAC findings.csv / findings_text.csv / notes_to_sefa.csv for the City's five audit years.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\fac_single_audit", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T19:30:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-CDGB-CV-CAPER-FUNDING-MAP", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Close out the complete funding map for the Cameron Lane Navigation Center using the City's HUD CAPER and Annual Action Plan filings", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "Discovered the City's entire HUD housing-document library through sitemap/City page enumeration and captured 21 documents (Con Plan, Annual Action Plans 2019-20 through 2024-25 with amendments, and CAPERs FY2019-20 through FY2025-26). DECISIVE FINDING - CDBG-CV, the City's own words: FY2020/21 CAPER p.3 states 'the City had a total of $2,159,775 in CARES Act funding (CDBG-CV) which, sans CDBG-CV Program Administration, was ENTIRELY ALLOCATED TO THE CAMERON LANE NAVIGATION CENTER OPERATIONS PROJECT. The Cameron Lane Navigation Center is an emergency homeless shelter located at 17631 Cameron Lane, Huntington Beach and served to protect homeless persons from contracting COVID-19...'. FY2022/23 CAPER p.1: '$2,028,847 to the Navigation Center with only $130,928 allocated to program administration... 893 homeless persons.' FY2023/24 CAPER p.1: 'all CDBG-CV funds have been expended... served 1,169 people since the pandemic started... 408 individuals, including 20 Veterans.' CDBG-CV came in two rounds: Round 1 $727,819 (FY2019/20 AAP Amendment No.2 'CARES Act Amendment') and Round 3 $1,431,956 notified 2020-09-11 (Amendment No.4 'CARES Act Amendment 2'), cumulative $2,159,775 - matching the HUD award B-20-MW-06-0506 reported to USAspending to the dollar. Amendment No.2 states in terms that the money funds 'the public service operations of an emergency homeless shelter located at 17631 Cameron Lane'. CRITICAL: CDBG-CV is a public-service grant that funded OPERATIONS ONLY and could not have paid for construction - this is the SECOND independent confirmation (after the FAC SEFA in TASK-20261018-FAC-SINGLE-AUDIT-SEFA) that the construction money was CRF/General-fund sourced, not CDBG-CV and not FEMA. COMPLETE FUNDING MAP now established: acquisition $5,863,000 LMIHAF; construction $5,610,000 Grants SRF (CARES/CRF); shelter operations 2020-2023 $2,028,847 CDBG-CV + $130,928 admin; supportive services $2,190,675 HOME-ARP; operating subsidy $3,290,000 SB2 over 5 years; FEMA PA (all DR-4482 programmes) $4,407,256 recognised. ALSO FOUND: HOME-ARP pairs the Navigation Center with 'the City's Project Homekey development, HB Oasis Project' - a SECOND Homekey project beyond the 17251 Beach Blvd motel application in Legistar 21-940. NEW RECORD-INTEGRITY FINDING (OBSTR-HBNC-NAME-2026-10-18-14): three DISTINCT facilities are all recorded as 'the Navigation Center' - 15161 Jackson Street SRO (7 units, HUD DH-2), 15311 Pipeline Lane emergency shelter (awarded $499,275 of CDBG in FY2018/19, 'on hold pending litigation', later sold 'resulting in a deficit of approximately $60,000'), and 17631 Cameron Lane. An analyst joining the documents could mis-attribute the $499,275 to the Cameron Lane site. CONTRACTS CAPTURED AND OCR'd: AESCO on-call materials testing/inspection - original agreement 2019-11-19 sum $250,000, Amendment No.1 (Legistar 19-1125, approved 2019-12-16) added $350,000 for a new not-to-exceed $600,000; and the 2021-12-21 on-call award (Legistar 21-845) where AESCO ranked #1 of 25 vendors. AESCO's contract is a unit-price on-call instrument, NOT site-specific, so the Cameron Lane testing ran through task orders and the contract alone does not price the shelter work. EEC Environmental (Environmental Engineering Contracting, Inc.): original agreement 2017-02-06, Amendment No.1 (Legistar 19-1189) extended term to 2021-02-06 with compensation not to exceed $375,000, followed by a new 3-year as-needed environmental engineering contract approved 2021-02-01 (Legistar 21-061) at $600,000 - the chain that carried EEC through the shelter period. EEC contract and both amendments OCR'd from image-only scans. Dossier gains section 4B (complete funding map + verbatim CAPER quotations + the naming-collision table); package now 100 files / 209 MB, hashed and mirrored x3 with verification.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\city_docs\\caper and ...\\contracts", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T20:15:00.000000Z"}
